title: The CLI builds its own WWW::ARDB client, so no command can be tested offline
updated: 2026-10-25T05:49:56Z
---

`WWW::ARDB::CLI::_build_api` constructs a fresh `WWW::ARDB` instance on every access:

    sub _build_api {
        my $self = shift;
        return WWW::ARDB->new(
            debug     => $self->debug,
            use_cache => !$self->no_cache,
        );
    }

The attribute is `is => 'lazy'` with a builder, so it is already overridable — but only by reaching past the CLI: a test would have to replace the builder or override the method, and the CLI offers no constructor argument for a client.

Consequence: `t/20-cli.t` cannot exercise the command classes against a mock user agent. Either it only checks argument parsing, or it reaches into the object, or it hits the live API — which is exactly what the offline fixture setup exists to prevent.

What to do:
1. Make the client injectable: an attribute on `WWW::ARDB::CLI` that, when given, is used as the client instead of building a fresh one. Keep the builder as the default path so the current behaviour is unchanged.
2. Check how `t/20-cli.t` currently obtains its data and write a test that runs at least one command class (`items` and `item` are the interesting ones: one list, one detail) against the mock user agent / fixtures in `t/fixtures/`, and asserts on the printed output.
3. The command classes reach the client through `$chain->[0]->api`; that path must keep working unchanged.

The global flags (`--debug`, `--json`, `--no-cache`) are parsed in `around BUILDARGS` with Getopt::Long in pass_through mode — do not break that while touching the constructor.

A behavioural change needs a test that pins it. Report the diff and the test names; no `Changes` entry is needed because the public behaviour of the CLI does not change.

